{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01B9Rc1WrxVRJvkmDHksENQL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context carefully to understand what needs to be done.\n\n## Project Summary\n\nThis is a Go reimplementation of GNU coreutils/moreutils utilities, verified via differential testing. The project uses a specification-driven development approach where SRDs define requirements, and an AI code generation pipeline (cobbler) generates Go implementations.\n\n## Current State\n\nLooking at the `requirement_states` in the project context, I can see which SRDs have requirements in \"ready\" state. Let me also check the `code_status` to understand what's implemented and what's not.\n\nFrom the code_status, the releases and their implementation status:\n\n\n- **00.0** (foundational infrastructure): partial - some use cases implemented, some not started (format, testutils)\n- **01.1** (cat): none - not started\n- **01.2** (sponge): all implemented\n- **01.3** (du): all implemented\n- **02.0** (true, false, yes, basename, dirname): all implemented\n- **02.1** (tee): all implemented\n- **02.2** (head, seq): all implemented\n- **03.0** (wc): all implemented\n- **03.1** (uname, arch, nproc, hostname, hostid): partial - n\nproc not started\n- **04.0** (ls): none - not started\n- **04.1** (realpath, readlink, pwd, tty, logname): partial - logname not started\n- **05.0** (echo, tac, nl, fold): partial - echo and tac not started\n- **05.1** (expand, unexpand): partial - expand not started\n- **05.2** (cut, paste): partial - cut not started\n- **05.3** (uniq, comm): all implemented\n- **05.4** (md5sum, sha1sum, sha256sum, sha512sum): partial - sha1sum and sha512sum not started\n- **06.0** (mkdir, rmdir, mktemp, ln, unlink): partial - mktemp not started\n- **06.1** (env, printenv, id, whoami, groups): partial - groups not started\n- **07.0** (sort, tr, tail\n\n): partial - sort not started\n- **07.1** (cp, mv, rm): partial - mv not started\n- **08.0** (date, sleep, touch, timeout, shuf, factor, expr): partial - sleep, touch, factor, expr not started\n- **08.1** (split, csplit, join, fmt, numfmt, od, printf): none\n- **09.0** (sha224sum, sha384sum, b2sum, cksum, sum): none\n- **09.1** (base32, base64, basenc): none\n- **10.0** (stat, truncate, link, sync): none\n- **10.1** (chmod, chgrp, chown): none\n- **11.0** (mkfifo, mknod, nice, nohup): none\n- **11.1** (users, who, pinky): none\n- **12.0** (sh): none\n\nLooking at the implementation status across all the command groups, I'm tracking which utilities are fully done, partially implemented, or haven't been started yet.\n\nNow I'm checking the requirement states and seeing which SRDs have requirements marked as ready—srd004-ts has many ready requirements, while srd062-touch, srd065-factor, srd066-expr, and srd067-split each have several ready requirements across different ranges.\n\nContinuing through the list, I notice that most of the remaining SRDs from srd068 onward have either the full R1.1-R4.4 range ready or many requirements ready, covering utilities like csplit, join, fmt, numfmt, od, printf, various checksum tools, base encoding utilities, stat, truncate, link, sync, and permission/ownership commands, along with user-related utilities and file system tools.\n\nNow I need to identify the earliest incomplete release and determine what tasks should be proposed for it. Looking at the release progression, I'm checking which releases have incomplete implementations—starting from 00.0 where format and testutils haven't been started, then 01.1 where cat hasn't been started, and continuing through the sequence to find where the first significant gap occurs.\n\nI'm realizing that the requirement_states list only shows SRDs with requirements currently in \"ready\" state, so if an SRD isn't listed, it either has all requirements completed or in progress, or it's not being tracked in this system. Looking at the recent commit history, I can see the generation pipeline is actively working through requirements in sequence, so the requirement_states gives me a clear picture of what's ready to be tackled next.\n\nFollowing the release-driven priority approach, I need to identify which releases have \"ready\" requirements by mapping each SRD to its corresponding release version. I'm checking through the SRD-to-release mappings to determine which release has the earliest incomplete work. Continuing through the release assignments for the remaining utilities, mapping each tool to its corresponding version from 11.0 through 13.0.\n\nNow I'm looking at the earliest release that has all its requirements satisfied—that's 08.0 with touch, factor, and expr—and checking what ready requirements each of those commands actually has.\n\nI need to verify the weight budgets for these requirements, but since I can't access the requirements.yaml file directly, I'm working with the pattern I'm seeing: the requirements appear to be grouped in sets of four (R3.1-R3.4 and R4.1-R4.4), which suggests they might already be organized into balanced groups.\n\nBased on the task structure from previous runs, each group of four requirements seems to fit within the weight budget of 4, so I'll proceed with proposing tasks using these natural groupings.\n\nLooking at the available requirements in release 08.0, I have five potential task groups across three commands, but I'm limited to four tasks maximum. I'll select the first four groups: touch with R3.1-R3.4, touch with R4.1-R4.4, factor with R4.1-R4.4, and expr with R3.1-R3.4. Now I need to verify whether any of these have dependencies that would affect the ordering.\n\nChecking the recent task patterns, I see that independent tasks like the timeout and shuf commands each target a single requirement group within their respective SRDs with no cross-dependencies. For touch, the R3 and R4 groups are both marked ready, which suggests the earlier requirement groups are already implemented. I should structure each task description to be self-contained and clear about what it covers.\n\nSince I can't directly access the SRD files, I'm working from the naming convention—srd062 for touch, srd065 for factor, srd066 for expr—where requirements are organized into numbered groups with four sub-requirements each. The task descriptions should follow the established pattern of specifying which requirement group is being addressed, and the required_reading should point to the appropriate SRD documentation.\n\nI'm laying out the first few tasks: the touch command covering requirement groups 3 and 4, then moving into the factor command. Each task identifies the specific files to modify and establishes dependencies between them so later work builds on earlier implementations.\n\nBut I need to reconsider—the constraint about not proposing tasks for external infrastructure shouldn't apply to the cmd/ directories since those are core components. Looking at the code status more carefully, even though touch and factor show \"not started,\" the fact that requirements R3 and R4 are ready suggests earlier work on R1 and R2 must already be complete, so there's existing code to build from. Looking at the weight budget constraints, each sub-requirement defaults to weight 1, so four requirements per group totals exactly 4, which fits the maximum. The recent task pattern confirms this—cmd/timeout handled R4.1-R4.4 as a single group. For srd070-fmt, the structure spreads across multiple requirement levels (R1.1 through R12.1), which suggests a different organization than the typical four-per-group pattern.\n\nNow I need to focus on the earliest release tasks and construct proper task descriptions with the right deliverable type, required reading, and file modifications.\n\nSince I can't access the actual SRD files, I should keep the task descriptions anchored to those documents and let the agent read them directly. I also need to be careful not to invent design decisions—those should come from the SRD itself, not my assumptions.\n\nLet me start drafting the four tasks for the 08.0 release, making sure each one is independently executable with just the task description and the SRD as reference material.\n\nNow I'm checking the weight distribution across tasks to ensure no single task exceeds the capacity limit. Each task covers four requirements with a weight of 1 each, totaling 4 per task, which fits within the constraint. I'm also considering the dependency structure—since requirement groups typically build sequentially, I'll have the later R4 requirements depend on the earlier R3 requirements within each utility.\n\nFor cross-utility dependencies, factor and expr are independent of each other and of touch since they're separate command-line tools. I've confirmed I'm at exactly 4 tasks, which meets the constraint, and since these packages already have earlier requirements completed, no contract task is needed.\n\nNow I'm preparing the output and thinking through what resources the stitch agent will need to reference: the SRD file, the existing main.go and test files for each command, the utility patterns in pkg/testutils and pkg/sys, and the project documentation. I'm realizing I need to actually reference the SRD requirement IDs in my task descriptions rather than making assumptions about what each group covers. Since I can't read the full SRD details, I should structure the requirements to map back to those specific IDs and let the agent handle the implementation details. I'm reconsidering how to structure the requirements so the stitch agent can understand what needs implementing. Looking at the recent commit patterns, tasks reference specific SRDs and requirement ranges like \"srd063 R4.1-R4.4\", so I should keep the requirements generic and point the agent to the SRD document itself rather than duplicating the full requirement text. This approach is cleaner and ensures the agent has the authoritative source.\n\nFor the task dependencies, I need to think through which tasks can run in parallel. Tasks for touch R3, factor R4, and expr R3 are all independent since they modify different files, so they can execute concurrently. Only the touch R4 task depends on touch R3 since they both modify the same command implementation.\n\nNow I'm re-examining the constraints around requirement counts. The planning constitution mentions 5-8 requirements per code task, but there's also a limit of 4 SRD sub-requirements per task. I need to reconcile these—it seems the SRD sub-requirements are a subset of the broader requirements, so I can have additional requirements beyond just the SRD references. I'm realizing there's a tension between P9's requirement that each SRD requirement maps to exactly one task requirement, and the guideline that code tasks should target 5-8 requirements. With a maximum of 4 SRD sub-requirements per task, the hard constraint of one-to-one mapping means I should have exactly 4 task requirements, not 5-8—so the range is more of a general target that gets overridden by the stricter SRD limitation.\n\nI'll structure each task with 4 requirements (one per SRD sub-requirement) and 4-5 acceptance criteria, plus 2-3 design decisions that tie back to the SRD and existing patterns.\n\nNow I'm mapping out the file structure for the three commands—touch, factor, and expr each have their main implementation and test files already in place from earlier requirements.\n\nLooking at the recent commit pattern, these tasks seem to implement requirements and tests together in single commits, which conflicts with the planning guideline about splitting implementation and tests. But given the 4-task weight limit and needing to cover multiple utilities, I think the practical constraint overrides the strict separation rule. The recent stitch tasks do modify both main.go and _test.go files in one task, so I'll follow that pattern here.\n\nI'm settling on a 4-task structure where each task covers a requirement group with both implementation and test modifications, keeping the descriptions self-contained and focused on what each task accomplishes.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":49778,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":49778},"output_tokens":33,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c539f765-d132-4e00-8c4d-8aef6dc161f7","uuid":"b1f07726-d46a-43b9-9293-bf690dfc610f"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01B9Rc1WrxVRJvkmDHksENQL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: go-unix-utils reimplements GNU coreutils/moreutils in Go, verified by differential testing against Homebrew reference binaries. The goal is to stress-test a spec-driven AI code generation pipeline.\n\n**Architecture**: SRDs define requirements per utility. The cobbler pipeline (measure + stitch) generates Go code in `cmd/` and `pkg/`. Each `cmd/<name>/` contains `main.go` and `<name>_test.go` with differential tests via `pkg/testutils.RunDiffTests`.\n\n**Current state**: Many utilities are partially or fully implemented. The generation pipeline is actively processing requirement groups — recent commits show srd063 (timeout) R4.1-R4.4 and srd064 (shuf) R4.1-R4.4 completed.\n\n**Earliest release with ready requirements**: Release 08.0 has ready requirements in srd062-touch (R3.1-R3.4, R4.1-R4.4), srd065-factor (R4.1-R4.4), and srd066-expr (R3.1-R3.4, R4.1-R4.4).\n\n## Priority Reasoning\n\nRelease 08.0 is the earliest incomplete release with \"ready\" requirements. Within 08.0:\n- **srd062-touch**: 2 requirement groups ready (R3, R4) — earlier groups already processed\n- **srd065-factor**: 1 requirement group ready (R4) — earlier groups already processed\n- **srd066-expr**: 2 requirement groups ready (R3, R4) — earlier groups already processed\n\nNo cross-utility dependencies. Touch R4 depends on touch R3 (same file modifications).\n\n## Weight Budget Check\n\n```\nTask 0: cmd/touch srd062 R3.1(w=1) + R3.2(w=1) + R3.3(w=1) + R3.4(w=1) = 4 ≤ 4 ✓\nTask 1: cmd/touch srd062 R4.1(w=1) + R4.2(w=1) + R4.3(w=1) + R4.4(w=1) = 4 ≤ 4 ✓\nTask 2: cmd/factor srd065 R4.1(w=1) + R4.2(w=1) + R4.3(w=1) + R4.4(w=1) = 4 ≤ 4 ✓\nTask 3: cmd/expr srd066 R3.1(w=1) + R3.2(w=1) + R3.3(w=1) + R3.4(w=1) = 4 ≤ 4 ✓\n```\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"[stitch] cmd/touch: srd062 R3.1-R3.4\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/software-requirements/srd062-touch.yaml (requirement group R3 -- implementation and test requirements)\n      - cmd/touch/main.go (existing implementation from earlier requirement groups)\n      - cmd/touch/touch_test.go (existing differential tests)\n      - pkg/testutils/difftest.go (DiffTest and RunDiffTests pattern)\n      - pkg/sys/stat.go (FileInfo, Stat, Lstat for timestamp operations)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/touch/main.go\n        action: modify\n        note: Implement srd062 R3.1-R3.4 functionality\n      - path: cmd/touch/touch_test.go\n        action: modify\n        note: Add differential tests for R3.1-R3.4\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Implement srd062-touch requirement R3.1 as specified in the SRD\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Implement srd062-touch requirement R3.2 as specified in the SRD\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Implement srd062-touch requirement R3.3 as specified in the SRD\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Implement srd062-touch requirement R3.4 as specified in the SRD\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Follow the differential testing pattern: use testutils.BuildBinary and testutils.RunDiffTests with reference binary gtouch\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Call sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() at the start of main per project convention\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"Use exec.LookPath for gtouch and t.Skip if not found, so tests pass on machines without Homebrew GNU binaries\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"srd062 R3.1 through R3.4 are implemented in cmd/touch/main.go\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"Differential tests for R3.1-R3.4 are added to cmd/touch/touch_test.go\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"go build -o bin/touch ./cmd/touch/ compiles without errors\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"go test ./cmd/touch/... -count=1 passes (or skips if gtouch not in PATH)\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: \"go vet ./cmd/touch/... reports no issues\"\n\n- index: 1\n  title: \"[stitch] cmd/touch: srd062 R4.1-R4.4\"\n  dependency: 0\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/software-requirements/srd062-touch.yaml (requirement group R4 -- implementation and test requirements)\n      - cmd/touch/main.go (existing implementation including R3.1-R3.4 from previous task)\n      - cmd/touch/touch_test.go (existing differential tests)\n      - pkg/testutils/difftest.go (DiffTest and RunDiffTests pattern)\n      - pkg/sys/stat.go (FileInfo, Stat, Lstat for timestamp operations)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/touch/main.go\n        action: modify\n        note: Implement srd062 R4.1-R4.4 functionality\n      - path: cmd/touch/touch_test.go\n        action: modify\n        note: Add differential tests for R4.1-R4.4\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Implement srd062-touch requirement R4.1 as specified in the SRD\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Implement srd062-touch requirement R4.2 as specified in the SRD\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Implement srd062-touch requirement R4.3 as specified in the SRD\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Implement srd062-touch requirement R4.4 as specified in the SRD\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Follow the differential testing pattern: use testutils.BuildBinary and testutils.RunDiffTests with reference binary gtouch\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Call sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() at the start of main per project convention\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"Use exec.LookPath for gtouch and t.Skip if not found, so tests pass on machines without Homebrew GNU binaries\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"srd062 R4.1 through R4.4 are implemented in cmd/touch/main.go\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"Differential tests for R4.1-R4.4 are added to cmd/to